The negative predictive value is calculated as:

\[
P(\text{no disease}|\text{negative})=\frac{\text{specificity}\times(1-\text{prevalence})}{\text{specificity}\times(1-\text{prevalence})+(1-\text{sensitivity})\times\text{prevalence}}
\]

\[
=\frac{0.80 \times 0.70}{0.80 \times 0.70 + 0.10 \times 0.30}
=\frac{0.56}{0.59}
\approx 0.949 \approx 95\%
\]

FINAL ANSWER: C. 95%

The probability that a person with a negative test truly has no disease is the negative predictive value:

\[
NPV = \frac{\text{True Negatives}}{\text{True Negatives} + \text{False Negatives}}
= \frac{0.70 \times 0.80}{(0.70 \times 0.80) + (0.30 \times 0.10)}
= \frac{0.56}{0.56 + 0.03}
= \frac{0.56}{0.59}
\approx 0.949 \approx 95\%
\]

FINAL ANSWER: C. 95%

