For an electrochemical reaction,

\[
\Delta G = -nFE
\]

For a one-electron process, \(n=1\) and \(F=96485\ \text{C mol}^{-1}\). Using the actual free energy change:

\[
E = -\frac{\Delta G}{nF}
= -\frac{-16.6\times 10^3\ \text{J}}{(1)(96485\ \text{C mol}^{-1})}
= 0.172\ \text{V}
\]

The corresponding standard potential is:

\[
E^\circ = -\frac{\Delta G^\circ}{nF}
= \frac{15.2\times 10^3}{96485}
= 0.158\ \text{V}
\]

So the actual potential is \(0.172\ \text{V}\), about \(0.0145\ \text{V}\) more positive than standard.

FINAL ANSWER: \(E = 0.172\ \text{V}\) (standard \(E^\circ = 0.158\ \text{V}\); shift \(= 0.0145\ \text{V}\)).

Use \(\Delta G = -nFE\) and \(\Delta G^\circ = -nFE^\circ\), with \(n=1\) and \(F = 96.485\ \text{kJ mol}^{-1}\text{V}^{-1}\).

\[
E^\circ = -\frac{\Delta G^\circ}{nF}
= -\frac{-15.2}{96.485}
= 0.158\ \text{V}
\]

\[
E = -\frac{\Delta G}{nF}
= -\frac{-16.6}{96.485}
= 0.172\ \text{V}
\]

FINAL ANSWER: \(E^\circ = 0.158\ \text{V}\) and \(E = 0.172\ \text{V}\) for the one-electron reaction.

Use \(\Delta G = -nFE\) with \(n=1\) and \(F = 96.485\ \text{kJ mol}^{-1}\text{V}^{-1}\).

\[
E^\circ = -\frac{\Delta G^\circ}{nF}
= \frac{15.2}{96.485}
= 0.158\ \text{V}
\]

\[
E = -\frac{\Delta G}{nF}
= \frac{16.6}{96.485}
= 0.172\ \text{V}
\]

FINAL ANSWER: \(E^\circ = +0.158\ \text{V}\), and the reaction potential \(E = +0.172\ \text{V}\).

